-- title:
--   Endomorphisms descend uniquely to the connected quotient Hopf algebra
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$, $G_c$, $G_e$ be commutative rings carrying Hopf algebra structures over $\mathbb{F}_p = \mathbb{Z}/p$ whose comultiplications are cocommutative and which are finite as $\mathbb{F}_p$-modules. Let $q_c : B \to G_c$ and $\sigma : G_e \to B$ be bialgebra homomorphisms over $\mathbb{F}_p$. Assume that $G_c$ is a local ring, that $G_e$ is reduced, that $q_c$ is surjective, and that the kernel of $q_c$, viewed as an algebra homomorphism, equals the ideal of $B$ generated by the image under $\sigma$ of the augmentation ideal of $G_e$, that is, of the kernel of the counit $\varepsilon_{G_e} : G_e \to \mathbb{F}_p$ regarded as an algebra homomorphism. Then for every bialgebra endomorphism $g$ of $B$ over $\mathbb{F}_p$ there is exactly one bialgebra endomorphism $g_c$ of $G_c$ over $\mathbb{F}_p$ satisfying $g_c \circ q_c = q_c \circ g$. Uniqueness is part of the assertion, so the descended endomorphism is determined by $g$.
--
--   In the dual language of finite commutative group schemes over $\mathbb{F}_p$, this is the statement that the identity component $\operatorname{Spec} G_c$ of $G = \operatorname{Spec} B$ is preserved by every endomorphism of $G$, so that endomorphisms act functorially on the connected part of the connected–étale splitting. It is used in the analysis of the ordinary part of the $p$-divisible group attached to a Jacobian, where the Hecke operator $U$, the diamond operators and the Verschiebung are compared on the connected quotient.
